No, AutoMapper does not have a built-in caching mechanism for objects. It simply maps the source to the destination based on the configuration provided.

However, you can implement a caching strategy yourself. Here's a simple example using a Dictionary:

```csharp
var cache = new Dictionary<Type, object>();

public void MapObjects()
{
    // Assuming your source is an array of type 'YourType'
    var source = new[] { new YourType() };

    foreach (var item in source)
    {
        if (!cache.ContainsKey(item.GetType()))
        {
            cache[item.GetType()] = Mapper.Map<YourType, YourDestinationType>(item);
        }
        else
        {
            var mappedObject = cache[item.GetType()];
            // Do something with the cached mapped object
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we're using a Dictionary to cache the mapped objects by their type. Before mapping an object, we check if it already exists in the cache. If it does, we use the cached version; otherwise, we map it and add it to the cache.

