The initialization of int in map<string, int> is not guaranteed to be zero. In C++, the default constructor for std::map (which is used when you declare a map without explicitly calling a constructor) initializes the elements to some default value, but this default value is not specified and can vary between different implementations of the standard library. For example, in some implementations, it might be zero, but in others, it could be some arbitrary value or even uninitialized memory. Therefore, relying on this behavior to initialize your data is not portable or reliable. Instead, you should explicitly initialize the elements of your map to zero (or whatever default value you desire) after constructing it. Here's how you can do it:

```cpp
map<string, int> count;
// ... (initialization code)

for (auto& pair : count) {
    pair.second = 0;  // Explicitly initialize each element to zero
}
```

This ensures that your program behaves consistently across different platforms and compiler implementations.

